# Variable policy

- @gixy/core/variable_catalog.py materializes a fresh catalog for each
  @gixy/core/manager.py audit. @gixy/core/context.py child contexts share that
  catalog; custom policy must never accumulate in process-global state.
- Layers: bundled < vendor < administrator < explicit directories in order.
  Choose highest matching layer, then exact name, then longest `_` prefix.
  Sorted `.cfg`/`.conf` filenames and later valid lines determine duplicates.
  Keep source path, line, and layer. Config-defined variables win first.
- @gixy/core/builtin_variables.py describes possible values, not module loading.
  Keep exact `brotli_ratio` as `[0-9.]*` to exclude CR/LF while retaining SSRF
  analysis. Never introduce a blanket safe module prefix.
- Ordinary quotes are literal, without script expansion, evaluation or Python
  unescaping. Raw quotes and legacy unquoted values are regexes. Validate regex
  syntax statically; never execute supplied regexes against strings. Missing
  or invalid entries warn and retain lower valid definitions. Empty/none/null
  are explicit trusted assertions, not malformed-value fallbacks.
- Automatic system policy requires root ownership, no group/world write bits,
  regular files, safe ancestors, and no symlinks. Hold no-follow descriptors
  through validation and reads. Missing optional directories are quiet; scans
  create none. Explicit directories are operator-selected trust, support `~`,
  and work for non-root users with actionable warnings on failure.
- @gixy/cli/argparser.py uses one alias-aware tri-state DefaultVarsAction for
  `--load-default-vars`/`--no-default-vars`. Canonical config/env setting is
  `load-default-vars`/`GIXY_LOAD_DEFAULT_VARS`. Preserve CLI > env > config > mode,
  including false and write-config roundtrips. Reject opposite CLI flags.
- CLI defaults to system discovery; library/server default false. Server startup
  passes vars_dirs/load_default_vars to Config; request data cannot change them.
  Append new Config arguments to retain positional compatibility. Python 3.6
  remains supported. Do not claim general analyzer thread safety.
- Tests: @tests/core/test_layered_variables.py,
  @tests/core/test_variable_policy_trust.py, @tests/cli/test_variable_policy.py.
  Container-only @scripts/verify_variable_layers_linux.py proves installed
  no-argument CLI behavior; never run its filesystem mutations on the host.

- @gixy/directives/block.py Geoip2Block exposes `declared_variables`, never
  ordinary `provide_variables`. Manager finalizes direct HTTP/stream-main aliases
  before sibling compilation; final GeoIP2 duplicate wins. Metadata narrowing
  requires the exact documented two-argument form. Lookup paths/defaults do not
  constrain arbitrary database UTF8/BYTES. Skip invalid inner includes before I/O.
- Mixed GeoIP2/map/geo/njs/Perl main-scope destinations use audit-owned exact
  node/name overrides, unrestricted output and all declaration locations. Ignore
  captures and map source keys. Local set overrides retain existing scope behavior.
- Context local named keys are lowercase; preserve numeric captures, ctx keys,
  original catalog lookup spelling and catalog case behavior.
- Script provider output is `[\s\S]*`, including writable js_var regardless
  of initializer. Preserve set_by_lua_block header args and uninterpreted raw body;
  do not execute scripts or read Lua files. njs nocache arity predates the
  bundled crossplane table, so parser validates this pinned form directly.
- @gixy/plugins/missing_resolver.py follows real dependencies to prove literals;
  configuration-defined dynamic host values require resolver. Catalog-only names
  retain their unsupported behavior. Never classify stringified regexes as
  hostnames.
- Update @docs/en/module-variable-coverage.md with reviewed source versions for
  registration and value-returning functions, defaults, scope, user-controlled
  characters and regression proof for each newly supported module variable.
  @tests/core/test_module_variables.py exercises these guarantees without
  NGINX, module loading, databases or script execution.

- In user-facing documentation, say "supported variables", "variable definitions"
  or "possible values" as appropriate; avoid "models" jargon. Explain numbers,
  fixed status strings and values that may contain any characters, including
  line breaks. Recognizing a name promises neither safety nor module availability.
